{"graph_chars": "A price ceiling is a legal maximum price. Governments use it when voters want a necessity kept cheap. Rent control is the book's case. Original equilibrium: five hundred dollars a month, fifteen thousand apartments. Demand for the neighborhood rises. Without a ceiling, rent would go to six hundred and seventeen thousand units. The city freezes rent at five hundred. Quantity supplied stays fifteen thousand. Quantity demanded jumps to nineteen thousand. Shortage of four thousand apartments. Irony: fewer apartments are rented under the ceiling than at the free-market rent. Landlords convert to co-ops, spend less on heat, hot water, paint. You do not get something for nothing. Cheaper housing is often worse housing. The people who do get a cheap unit gain. The people who cannot find one, and the landlords, lose.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0623], [0.0623, 0.1245], [0.1245, 0.1868], [0.1868, 0.2491], [0.2491, 0.3114], [0.3114, 0.3736], [0.3736, 0.4359], [0.4359, 0.4982], [0.4982, 0.5604], [0.5604, 0.6227], [0.6227, 0.685], [0.685, 0.7473], [0.7473, 0.8095], [0.8095, 0.8718], [0.8718, 0.9341], [0.9341, 0.9963], [0.9963, 1.0586], [1.0586, 1.1209], [1.1209, 1.1832], [1.1832, 1.2454], [1.2454, 1.3077], [1.3077, 1.37], [1.37, 1.4322], [1.4322, 1.4945], [1.4945, 1.5568], [1.5568, 1.619], [1.619, 1.6813], [1.6813, 1.7436], [1.7436, 1.8059], [1.8059, 1.8681], [1.8681, 1.9304], [1.9304, 1.9927], [1.9927, 2.0549], [2.0549, 2.1172], [2.1172, 2.1795], [2.1795, 2.2418], [2.2418, 2.304], [2.304, 2.3663], [2.3663, 2.4286], [2.4286, 2.4908], [2.4908, 2.5531], [2.5531, 2.6154], [2.6154, 2.6777], [2.6777, 2.7399], [2.7399, 2.8022], [2.8022, 2.8645], [2.8645, 2.9267], [2.9267, 2.989], [2.989, 3.0513], [3.0513, 3.1136], [3.1136, 3.1758], [3.1758, 3.2381], [3.2381, 3.3004], [3.3004, 3.3626], [3.3626, 3.4249], [3.4249, 3.4872], [3.4872, 3.5495], [3.5495, 3.6117], [3.6117, 3.674], [3.674, 3.7363], [3.7363, 3.7985], [3.7985, 3.8608], [3.8608, 3.9231], [3.9231, 3.9853], [3.9853, 4